---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import NpvCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/NpvCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="npv-calculator">
  <NpvCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the NPV calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The NPV calculator discounts each yearly cash flow to its value today and adds them up. A positive net
      present value means the project earns more than the discount rate, so it adds value at that rate. A
      negative NPV means it earns less.
    </p>
    <p>
      The formula is <code>NPV = Σ CFₜ ÷ (1 + r)^t</code>. <code>CFₜ</code> is the cash flow in year
      <code>t</code>, with year 0 today, and <code>r</code> is the discount rate. The profitability index is
      the present value of years 1 to <code>n</code> divided by the investment in year 0. The payback period
      is the time until the running total of the cash flows turns positive. The discounted payback period
      uses the present values.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A project costs 50,000 today and returns 12,000, 15,000, 16,000, 14,000 and 13,000 over 5 years. At an
      8% discount rate, the returns are worth 55,810.51 today, so the NPV is 5,810.51 and the profitability
      index is 1.116. The IRR is 12.27%. The cash pays back after 3.50 years; with discounting, after 4.34
      years. At 15% the NPV is −3,234.96.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Every cash flow after year 0 comes at the end of its year, and one discount rate applies to all years.
      Payback ignores the cash flows after the payback year. When the cash flows change sign more than once,
      the project can have more than one IRR.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
